      "heading": "Convolution: one small template, slid everywhere",
      "narration": "The fix for images is one of the most elegant ideas in the field, and it is smaller than people expect. Take a tiny grid of weights — three by three is the standard, so nine numbers. Lay that grid over the top-left corner of the image, multiply each weight by the pixel underneath it, add the nine products together, and write down the single number you get. That is a dot product, exactly the one from Part One, computed over a neighbourhood instead of over the whole image. Now slide the grid one pixel to the right and do it again. And again, across the row, then down. When you have covered the image you have produced a new, slightly smaller image. That grid of weights is called a kernel or a filter, and that sliding operation is a convolution. Here is why it fixes everything. It is local: each output looks only at a small patch, which is exactly the fact we said was true about images. And — this is the part to hold on to — the same nine numbers are used at every single position. That is called weight sharing, and it does two things at once. It means a feature learned anywhere is available everywhere: if those nine weights happen to detect a vertical edge, they detect vertical edges in the top-left and the bottom-right and the middle, automatically, with no extra training and no extra parameters. And it collapses the cost. Where the MLP needed a hundred and twenty thousand weights per neuron, this needs nine. Not nine thousand. Nine. One kernel gives you one output image, and that output is called a feature map, because what it really shows you is where in the original image that kernel's pattern occurs — bright where the pattern is present, dark where it is not. One pattern is not much, so a convolutional layer runs many kernels side by side, each with its own nine numbers, each learning to look for something different. Sixty-four kernels give you sixty-four feature maps stacked together, which you can think of as asking sixty-four different questions of every location in the image at once. That stack is what the next layer reads, so channels in and channels out is the shape you have to keep track of. Three more knobs and you have the whole operation. Stride is how far the kernel jumps between positions — a stride of one looks everywhere, a stride of two skips every other position and halves the size of the output. Padding is a ring of zeros around the edge of the input, which stops the image shrinking a little at every layer and stops the border pixels from being under-counted. And pooling, usually max pooling over a two by two square, is a deliberate downsampling step: keep the strongest response in each little region and throw the rest away. That last one is doing something important rather than just saving memory. Halving the resolution means the next layer's three by three kernel is looking at twice as much of the original image through the same small window, and stacking that is how a network built entirely out of three-pixel-wide filters ends up seeing whole objects.",
