      "heading": "Why RNNs lost, and why transformers felt inevitable",
      "narration": "So, the indictment. It really is two sentences, and everything in the next part of this course follows from them. The first is fading memory. Look again at the update rule: the state is multiplied by a weight matrix at every single timestep. To find out how something at step one influences the output at step three hundred, you multiply through three hundred matrices. We have seen exactly this before, in the vanishing gradient lesson — repeated multiplication by numbers below one drives a signal to zero, and repeated multiplication by numbers above one blows it up. Here the same thing happens along the time axis instead of along the depth of the network. In practice, a vanilla RNN struggles to connect information more than about ten steps apart. LSTM gates were built precisely to fight this, and they genuinely help — the gate can choose to leave a value alone rather than re-multiplying it — but they push the limit out to maybe a few hundred steps rather than removing it. For a paragraph that is fine. For a document, or a long conversation, or a codebase, it is not. The second sentence is about hardware, and it is the one that actually killed the architecture. The state at time t depends on the state at time t minus one. That dependency is unavoidable — it is the definition of recurrence. So a sequence of five hundred tokens is five hundred strictly sequential steps, each one waiting for the last. Meanwhile you are running this on a graphics card with thousands of cores designed to do thousands of things simultaneously, and almost all of them are idle, because the architecture forbids you from using them. Convolution does not have this problem: every patch of an image is independent, so a CNN saturates a GPU beautifully. An RNN cannot. And in a field where progress has been driven by scale — more data, bigger models, more compute — an architecture that structurally cannot be parallelized is an architecture that cannot ride the hardware curve, no matter how good it is. Now put the two complaints side by side, because the solution is a single idea that answers both. If the problem is that information degrades while being copied forward, and that you are forced to walk through every intermediate step to get anywhere, then stop copying it forward and stop walking. Let every position look directly at every other position, weight those looks by how relevant each one is, and read what you need in a single step. Nothing is passed hand to hand, so nothing fades. There is no dependency chain, so the entire sequence computes at once and the GPU is finally busy. That is attention, that is the whole content of the twenty-seventeen paper called Attention Is All You Need, and the title is a fair description. One last note so the lesson is honest. CNNs did not lose in the way RNNs did. Convolution is still what runs in your phone camera, in medical scanners, in real-time detection on small devices — it is efficient and it is right about images, and even the vision transformers that beat it borrowed its patch-based structure. It is sequences where the story ends here. The next lesson opens that door.",
